  "screenwriter_raw": "{\n  \"pseudos\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"p1\",\n      \"text\": \"After a casting decision placed the actor cast in the roles of Helen of Troy and Clytemnestra in Christopher Nolan's upcoming Homer adaptation, a fierce online backlash erupted. Those who stood up for the casting faced digital backlash from accusers attacking the production, who leveled a public accusation of erasing European history. The defenders argued that diverse storytelling honors shared cultural heritage, but the dispute has turned the mythic epic into a flashpoint over identity and belonging.\",\n      \"fit\": 0.92,\n      \"center\": \"who-3\",\n      \"channel\": \"toned\",\n      \"source\": {\n        \"fragments\": [\"how-0\", \"how-1\", \"how-2\", \"how-3\", \"result-0\"]\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"p2\",\n      \"text\": \"The cultural controversy ignited by this casting has forced a confrontation on a new battleground. On the internet, protectors of diverse storytelling stood their ground against hostile reactions online, defending the moral stance that the Western canon is shared civilizational heritage. This battle over representation in film is a direct challenge to those who would claim classical stories belong only to one history.\",\n      \"fit\": 0.85,\n      \"center\": \"result-0\",\n      \"channel\": \"toned\",\n      \"source\": {\n        \"fragments\": [\"why-1\", \"how-1\", \"result-1\"]\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"p3\",\n      \"text\": \"In response to critics of the casting, a counter-argument was made in defense of the artistic choice. This defense statement asserted that inclusive storytelling is not erasure but evolution, treating the Western canon as shared civilizational heritage for a modern audience. The fight now extends beyond the film to a larger public debate over who gets to tell these stories.\",\n      \"fit\": 0.78,\n      \"center\": \"how-3\",\n      \"channel\": \"toned\",\n      \"source\": {\n 